hadiths[54]{hadithnumber,text}:
"607","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: When you see the (crescent of) Ramadan, then fast, and when you see the (crescent of) Shawwal, then break the fast. If it is cloudy, then estimate. And Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to fast one day before the crescent of Ramadan. Ibrahim bin Sa’d was asked: Did he do this to welcome (Ramadan)? He said: Yes."
"608","It is narrated from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: The month can also be of twenty-nine days. Do not begin fasting until you see the new moon, and do not break the fast until you see (the Shawwal) moon. And if the sky is cloudy, then complete the count to thirty."
"609","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Do not fast one or two days before the beginning of Ramadan, except for a person who habitually fasts on that day. Begin fasting upon sighting the (Ramadan) crescent, and break the fast upon sighting the (Shawwal) crescent. If it is cloudy, then complete thirty days."
"610","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Do not fast one or two days before Ramadan, except for a person who is already in the habit of fasting, then he may fast on those days as well."
"611","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: I am astonished at those people who begin fasting even before the month (of Ramadan) starts. Whereas the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Do not fast until you see the crescent of Ramadan, and do not break your fast until you see the crescent (of Shawwal)."
"612","It is narrated from Fatimah bint Husayn that a man testified before Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) about sighting the crescent of Ramadan on the first night, so he fasted, and according to my understanding, he said that he also ordered the people to fast. And he said: For me to fast one day of Sha'ban is more beloved than to miss one day of Ramadan. Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) later said: For fasting in Ramadan, the testimony of two men (regarding the sighting of the crescent) is necessary."
"613","It is narrated from Humaid bin Abdur Rahman bin Awf that Umar and Uthman (may Allah be pleased with them) used to perform the Maghrib prayer when they saw the darkness of the night, and then after the prayer they would break their fast. This was during Ramadan."
"614","It is narrated from Sahl bin Sa’d Al-Sa’idi (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: People will remain upon goodness as long as they hasten in breaking the fast."
"615","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: Bilal gives the call to prayer while a portion of the night remains, so continue eating and drinking until Ibn Umm Maktum gives the call to prayer. Ibn Umm Maktum was a blind man; he would not give the call to prayer until it was said to him, ""You have entered the morning, you have entered the morning."
"616","It is narrated from Salim (may Allah have mercy on him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) gives the call to prayer while a part of the night remains, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum (may Allah be pleased with him) gives the call to prayer. And he was a blind man; he would not give the call to prayer until it was said to him: 'You have entered the morning, you have entered the morning.'"
"617","It is narrated from Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) set out towards Makkah in the month of Ramadan in the year of the conquest of Makkah, and he was fasting, and he instructed the people not to fast. It was said to him that when you fasted, the people also fasted. The Prophet (ﷺ) then called for a vessel of water, placed it on his hand, and told those in front of him to break their fast. When they had broken their fast, and those with them had also broken their fast, the Prophet (ﷺ) lifted the vessel, brought it to his mouth, and drank the water. In both of their narrations, or in the narration of someone else, it is mentioned that this happened after Asr."
"618","It is narrated from Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet ﷺ set out from Madinah until he reached the place of ""Kura‘ al-Ghamim,"" and he was fasting. Then he ﷺ raised a vessel of water and placed it on his hand while he was on his she-camel. Those in front of and behind him broke their fast, then he drank the water while the people were watching."
"619","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) set out in the year of the conquest of Makkah during Ramadan. He (peace be upon him) observed the fast until he reached the place of ""Kuraid,"" whereupon he broke his fast, and the people also broke their fast along with him. It was the habit of the noble Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) to adopt every new practice of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)."
"620","Abu Bakr bin Abdur Rahman narrates from some of the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the Prophet ﷺ, in the year of the conquest of Makkah, while on a journey, ordered the people to break their fast and said: “Gain strength to face your enemy.” And the Prophet ﷺ himself kept the fast. Abu Bakr bin Abdur Rahman says that the companion who narrated to me said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ at the place of ‘Arj pouring water over his head due to intense thirst or heat. It was said to him, O Messenger of Allah ﷺ! A group of people among the people have kept the fast when you kept the fast. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached the place of Kuraid, he asked for a cup and drank water from it, so the people also broke their fast."
"621","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) set out towards Makkah in the year of the conquest of Makkah during Ramadan. He (peace be upon him) kept the fast until he reached the place of ""Kura‘ al-Ghamim."" The people were also fasting with him. It was said to him (peace be upon him) that fasting had become difficult for the people. After ‘Asr, he (peace be upon him) called for a cup of water, drank it while the people were watching, and some broke their fast while others continued fasting. When he (peace be upon him) was informed that some people were still fasting, he (peace be upon him) said: ""They are disobedient."
"622","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that he reports: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) set out towards Makkah in the year of the conquest of Makkah during Ramadan, and he ordered the people to break their fast and said: ""Strengthen yourselves against your enemy."" It was said to him that some people refused to break their fast. When he (the Prophet) kept the fast, he called for a cup of water and drank from it. Then he narrated the rest of the hadith at length."
"623","Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that we were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the expedition of Tabuk, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would travel during the forenoon. Suddenly, he (ﷺ) saw a group of people under the shade of a tree, so he asked: ""What is this group?"" The people said: There is a fasting person among them whom fasting has put in difficulty (or he said something similar). So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Fasting while traveling is not an act of righteousness."
"624","It is narrated from Ka'b bin Asim Al-Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Fasting during travel is not a good deed."
"625","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that Hamza bin Amr Al-Aslami (may Allah be pleased with him) used to fast frequently. He said: O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)! Should I fast while traveling? The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""If you wish, then fast, and if you wish, then do not fast."
"626","It is narrated from Anas bin Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who states that we traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during Ramadan. The fasting person did not find fault with the one who was not fasting, nor did the one who was not fasting find fault with the fasting person."
"627","It is narrated from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who states: We traveled with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). Some of us were fasting and some were not. So the one who was fasting did not find fault with the one who was not fasting, and the one who was not fasting did not find fault with the one who was fasting."
"628","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to fast on the 10th of Muharram and would also command (others) to fast on this day."
"629","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: In the days of ignorance, the Quraysh used to fast on the day of Ashura, and the Prophet (ﷺ) also used to fast. When the Prophet (ﷺ) came to Madinah, he also fasted on that day and ordered the people to fast on that day. When fasting in Ramadan was made obligatory, these (fasts of Ramadan) became obligatory and he abandoned (the fast of the day of Ashura), and said: ""Whoever wishes may fast on this day, and whoever wishes may leave it."
"630","Humaid bin Abdur Rahman narrates that I heard Muawiyah bin Abi Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with him) on the day of Ashura while he was seated on the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He took out a handful of hair from his sleeve and was saying, ""O people of Medina! Where are your scholars? I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbidding such practices and saying, 'Indeed, the Children of Israel were destroyed when their women began to do this.'"" Then he said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ regarding this day, he said: 'I am fasting today, so whoever among you wishes may fast.'"
"631","It is narrated from Humaid bin Abdur Rahman that he heard Muawiyah bin Abi Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with him) in the year he performed Hajj, while he was delivering a sermon from the pulpit. He said: O people of Madinah! Where are your scholars? I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on this very day, the Day of Ashura, say: ""Fasting on this day has not been made obligatory by Allah upon you, but I am fasting. Whoever among you wishes may fast, and whoever wishes may break his fast."
"632","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he stated: The fast of the day of Ashura was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""The people of ignorance used to fast on this day. Whoever among you wishes to fast on this day may do so, and whoever dislikes it may leave it."
"633","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrates: I do not know that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) specifically intended to fast on any day other than the Day of Ashura."
"634","Narrated by Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her): She said that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to me and I said, ""We have kept hais (a type of sweet dish) for you."" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, ""I intended to fast, but bring it near."" (Let me eat it.)"
"635","Mother of the believers, Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) came to me. I said, ""We have prepared hais for you."" He said, ""I intended to fast, but bring it near."
"636","It is narrated from Sayyida Hafsa and Sayyida Aisha (may Allah be pleased with them) that they narrate from the Noble Prophet ﷺ that both of them (Hafsa and Aisha, may Allah be pleased with them) began the morning in a state of fasting. During the day, something was gifted to them, so both of them broke their fast, and they mentioned this to the Prophet ﷺ. He ﷺ said: ""Both of you should make up the fast of today on another day."" Ibn Jurayj (may Allah have mercy on him) says: I asked Ibn Shihab (may Allah have mercy on him): Did you hear this from Urwah bin Zubair (may Allah have mercy on him)? He said: No, rather, a man standing at the door of Abdul Malik bin Marwan or a man from the gathering of Abdul Malik bin Marwan informed me of this."
"637","It is narrated from Ata bin Abi Rabah (may Allah have mercy on him) that Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) did not consider it objectionable for a person to break a voluntary fast, and he used to give examples for this, saying: ""If a man performed seven circuits (of Tawaf) but did not complete them, then he will have the reward according to his intention; or if someone prayed one rak'ah but did not pray the second, then he will have the reward according to the intention for which he sought reward."
"638","Amr bin Dinar (may Allah have mercy on him) says that Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) did not see any harm in breaking a voluntary fast."
"639","It is narrated from Muhammad bin Sirin (may Allah have mercy on him) that his father invited a group of the noble Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) of the Prophet ﷺ to a wedding feast (walimah). The Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) came, and among them was Ubayy bin Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him). The narrator says: I think that Ubayy bin Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) supplicated for blessing and then left."
"640","Abdullah bin Abi Yazid (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates that my father invited Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both). He came and sat down, and the food was served. Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) extended his hand and said: ""Take (eat) in the name of Allah,"" and then Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) withdrew his hand and said: ""I am fasting."
"641","It is narrated from Anas bin Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ came to the house of Abu Talha Al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, and a group was with him ﷺ. They ate food at his place, and this was an invitation other than a wedding feast (walimah)."
"642","It is narrated from Abu Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) that he would come to his family when half the day had passed or a little before that, and would say: ""Is there any breakfast?"" If there was or was not, he would say: ""I will fast today,"" and then he would fast. If he was not fasting and came at that time, he would remain without fasting."
"643","Ata (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates that we were informed that they used to do the same: when they would begin the morning without fasting, until forenoon or after it, and perhaps they had found breakfast or perhaps not."
"644","It is narrated from ‘Ata bin Yasar (may Allah have mercy on him) that a man kissed his wife while he was fasting, and this caused him great distress. He sent his wife to inquire about this matter. The woman went to Umm al-Mu’minin, Sayyidah Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), and asked her. Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) replied, “The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to kiss (his wives) while fasting.” The woman returned and informed her husband, but this made him even more anxious. The man said, “We are not like the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), for Allah makes lawful for His Messenger whatever He wills.” The woman went again to Sayyidah Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) and found the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) present at her place. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) asked, “What is the matter with this woman?” Sayyidah Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) informed him, so he (ﷺ) said, “Did you not tell her that I do this?” Sayyidah Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) said, “I did inform her, but when she went back to her husband, he became even more distressed and said, ‘We are not like the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), for Allah makes lawful for His Messenger whatever He wills.’” Upon hearing this, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) became angry and said, “By Allah! I am the most God-fearing among you and the most knowledgeable of Allah’s limits.”"
"645","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) would kiss some of his wives while he was fasting, and then he would smile."
"646","It is narrated from Ata bin Yasar (may Allah have mercy on him) that Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) was asked about a fasting person kissing. He granted concession for the elderly and disliked it for the young."
"647","Narrated from Sayyida Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that a man said to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while she was listening: ""I wake up in the morning in a state of janabah (major ritual impurity) and I intend to fast."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I also wake up in the morning in a state of janabah and I intend to fast. I perform ghusl (ritual bath) and then fast that day."" The man said: ""You and we are not the same, because Allah has forgiven your past and future sins."" (Upon hearing this) the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) became angry and said: ""By Allah! I am the one among you who fears Allah the most, and I am the one among you who knows best the limits from which one must abstain."
"648","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that a man said to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was standing at the door and I was listening: ""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)! I wake up in a state of major ritual impurity (janabah) and I intend to fast."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I also wake up in a state of janabah and I intend to fast. I perform ghusl first, and then I fast that day."
"649","Abu Bakr bin Abdur Rahman narrates that I and my father were with the governor of Madinah, Marwan bin Hakam, when mention was made regarding Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he says: ""Whoever wakes up in a state of major ritual impurity (janabah) should break his fast that day."" Marwan said: O Abdur Rahman! I adjure you to go to the Mothers of the Believers, Lady Aisha and Lady Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with them both), and definitely ask them about this. Abu Bakr says: Abdur Rahman went and I also went with him until we came to Lady Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her). Abdur Rahman greeted her and said: O Mother of the Believers! We were with Marwan when it was mentioned before us that Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Whoever wakes up in a state of major ritual impurity (janabah) will break his fast that day."" Lady Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""O Abdur Rahman! The matter is not as Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) has said. Will you turn away from the practice of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)?"" Abdur Rahman said: By Allah! Certainly not, O Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her)! So Lady Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""I bear witness upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) that he would wake up in a state of major ritual impurity (janabah) due to intercourse, not due to a wet dream, and then he would fast that day."" Abu Bakr says: Then we left and came to Lady Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her). We asked her about this as well, and she said the same as Lady Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) had said. Abu Bakr says: We left until we came to Marwan. Abdur Rahman told him what both of them (the pure wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him) had said. So Marwan said: O Abu Muhammad! I adjure you to definitely mount the riding animal standing at the door and go to Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) and inform him about this. Abu Bakr says: Abdur Rahman mounted, and I also mounted with him, until we came to Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him). Abdur Rahman conversed with him for a while (about other matters), then mentioned this incident to him. So Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I have no knowledge of this; someone who informed me told me so."
"650","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would enter the morning in a state of major ritual impurity (janabah), then he would perform ghusl (ritual bath) and observe the fast of that day."
"651","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that a man broke his fast in Ramadan (by having intercourse), so the Prophet (peace be upon him) ordered him to free a slave, or to fast for two consecutive months, or to feed sixty needy people. The man said: ""I do not have the ability to do that."" A large basket of dates was brought to the Prophet (peace be upon him). He said: ""Take it and give it as charity."" The man said: ""There is no one more needy than me."" At this, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) laughed so much that his front teeth became visible, then he said: ""Alright, (go and) eat it yourself."" Imam Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""His fast was broken because of intercourse."
"652","Saeed bin Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates that a Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he was pulling out his hair and beating his chest, and saying: and he was beating his chest and saying: ""Separation has destroyed me."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: ""What happened?"" He said: ""I had intercourse with my wife while fasting in Ramadan."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: ""Do you have the ability to free a slave?"" He said: ""No."" He ﷺ asked: ""Do you have the ability to sacrifice a camel?"" He said: ""No."" He ﷺ said: ""Alright, sit down."" Saeed bin Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) says that a large bag of dates was presented to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he ﷺ said: ""Take this and give it in charity."" The man said: ""I do not find anyone more needy than myself."" He ﷺ said: ""Eat it, and fast another day in place of that (intercourse) day."" Ata (may Allah have mercy on him) says: I asked Saeed bin Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him): ""How many dates were in that bag?"" He replied: ""Between 15 and 20 sa’."
"653","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) was asked about a pregnant woman: what should she do if she fears for her child? He (may Allah be pleased with him) replied: She should not fast and for each day she should feed a poor person with one mudd of wheat."
"654","It is narrated from Shaddad bin Aws (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: During the conquest of Makkah, we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). He (peace be upon him) saw a man who had undergone cupping, while eighteen days of Ramadan had passed. The Prophet (peace be upon him) was holding my hand and said: ""Both the one who performs cupping and the one who gets cupped have broken their fast."
"655","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had cupping done while in the state of ihram and while fasting."
"656","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) that he reports from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) used to get cupped while fasting, then later he abandoned it."
"657","It is narrated from Khalid bin Aslam (may Allah have mercy on him) that Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) broke his fast on a cloudy day in Ramadan, thinking that perhaps evening had come and the sun had set. A man came and said, ""O Commander of the Faithful! The sun has risen,"" (meaning it had not yet set). So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""This matter is easy."
"658","Imam Shafi’i, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Whoever vomits intentionally while fasting, it is obligatory upon him to make up the fast. And whoever vomits unintentionally (i.e., does not do so deliberately), then making up the fast is not obligatory upon him."
"659","And likewise, Malik (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated to us from Nafi‘, who narrated from Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"660","It is narrated from Abu Salamah (may Allah have mercy on him) that he heard from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: I used to have to make up the missed fasts of Ramadan, but I would not have the ability to do so before Sha'ban. (That is, I would make them up in Sha'ban.)"
